  </div>


  <div class="impact-block brief-keep">
    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <ul class="impact-list">
      <li><b>Took GM Energy from zero to a launched commerce experience</b><span>Defined what the experience should be — service blueprint, personas, journeys — then carried it through wireframes, sprint delivery, and launch: configurable bundles, financing inside checkout, installation scheduling, and account management.</span></li>
      <li><b>Unified purchase, financing, and installation into one checkout</b><span>Took what began as a hand-off between GM Energy, GM Financial, and the install partner and integrated it into a single flow — purchasing the products, financing them with installation, and quoting the install in one process, so the customer is never passed back and forth. One of the hardest problems in the project, and it took many iterations to get right.</span></li>
      <li><b>Established the research framework for validation testing</b><span>Set the foundation for how the team tests: every study starts by defining what we're asking, why we're asking it, what the result should tell us — and how that answer will drive the design decision. Our senior UX designer then evolved that foundation into the mature, well-defined testing process the team runs today.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat"><b>0 → 1</b><span>No digital presence to a launched, financed, installable storefront</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>4 personas</b><span>Evidence-based, reshaping who the business designs for</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>7+ workstreams</b><span>Storefront · Checkout · Sales guide · Installation · Energy Genie · Executive dashboard · Dealership experience</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="brief-only">
    <h2>The challenge</h2>
    <p>In fall 2022, home energy management systems were an emerging category and GM was entering it for the first time. There was no ecommerce presence, no established journey, and no agreed definition of who the customer was. It started as a service design engagement: define how GM Energy should engage with and serve its customers, work out the systems and processes that would take, and produce the roadmap for building it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="brief-only">
    <h2>Who we designed for</h2>
    <div class="pstrip">
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/persona-av-markus.webp" alt="Markus" loading="lazy"><span class="seg">Tech Builder</span><b>Markus</b><p>Efficiency-minded project manager, frustrated by outages — wants visibility and control over home energy, considering an EV.</p></figure>
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/persona-av-tom.webp" alt="Tom" loading="lazy"><span class="seg">Shiny Elite</span><b>Tom</b><p>Finance executive and GM EV owner who wants the best of the best — premium products, reliable through blackouts.</p></figure>
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/persona-av-fiona.webp" alt="Fiona" loading="lazy"><span class="seg">Smart Tech</span><b>Fiona</b><p>Research-driven Lyriq owner weighing solar and backup power — values usability, warranties, and the best possible deal.</p></figure>
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/persona-av-rhonda.webp" alt="Rhonda" loading="lazy"><span class="seg">Smart &amp; Safe</span><b>Rhonda</b><p>Educator nearing retirement in blackout-prone Florida — wants dependable backup power and expert guidance, no EV interest.</p></figure>
    </div>
    <p>With no precedent to work from, the first personas were assumptions grounded in the closest adjacent customers — solar and backup-generator buyers. We validated them against stakeholder interviews and secondary research, then kept refining them for three years against sentiment pulled from every usability study.</p>
    <p>We discovered early on a group of buyers, represented by Rhonda, that may not be interested in EVs at all: people who want home energy security because extreme weather keeps knocking their power out. For a company built around vehicles, that meant customers who would never arrive through vehicle sales.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="brief-only">
    <h2>The major tracks</h2>
    <p>One engagement with several workstreams running at once, from 2022 through launch and after.</p>
    <div class="track-cards">
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot shot--scroll lg-drift"><img src="/assets/img/hems/live-home-desktop.webp" alt="The live GM Energy homepage" loading="lazy"></div><b>Storefront</b><span>Created the ecommerce experience — wireframed the site, went deep on home, landing and product pages, partnering with the UI team for delivery.</span></div>
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot shot--static"><img src="/assets/img/hems/checkout-flow-card.webp" alt="Checkout wireframe flow matrix — four purchase variants" loading="lazy" style="object-fit:contain;background:#fff"></div><b>Checkout &amp; financing</b><span>Unified purchase, GM Financial financing, and install quoting into one flow — the customer is never passed between three companies.</span></div>
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/fig-chart-quote-eligibility.webp" alt="Install quote eligibility step" loading="lazy"></div><b>Installation questionnaire</b><span>The step-by-step home assessment that turns an installation into a quote inside checkout.</span></div>
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot shot--genie"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/hems/genie-bg-desktop.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy"><div class="flyout" id="genie-shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie-conversation-card.webp" alt="Energy Genie 2.0 flyout — the assistant reads back what it heard, then moves into structured questions" loading="lazy"></div></div><b>Energy Genie</b><span>A shopping survey — answer the questions, get the products your answers point at. Genie 2.0 added an AI version you could ask directly, or have it ask you.</span></div>
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot shot--static"><img src="/assets/img/hems/salesguide-welcome.webp" alt="Sales guide welcome spread (sanitized)" loading="lazy"></div><b>Sales guide</b><span>Nobody asked for it — the site's journey in a form the sales team could use in a conversation. Adopted across the GME sales organization.</span></div>
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/dash-map-light.webp" alt="Dashboard map component exploration" loading="lazy"></div><b>Executive dashboard</b><span>A business-facing view of funnel and program health for leadership.</span></div>
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/journey-v2-2025.webp" alt="2025 journey map" loading="lazy"></div><b>Research &amp; testing</b><span>Blueprints, journeys, personas, storyboards — and the validation-testing framework the team still runs on.</span></div>
      <div class="tcard reveal"><div class="shot shot--future">Future-state concepts — in exploration</div><b>Dealership experience</b><span>Future-state concepts for how home energy gets sold in physical retail.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

<div class="act-kicker" id="part-01"><span>Part 01</span>Build the ground truth</div>
  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>In fall 2022, home energy management systems were an emerging category and GM was entering it for the first time. There was no ecommerce presence, no established journey, and no agreed definition of who the customer was.</p>
  <p>It started as a service design engagement with four goals: define how GM Energy should engage with and serve its customers; work out which systems, platforms and processes that would take; show the future state through concepts and prototypes; and produce the roadmap for building it.</p>
  <h2>Research: understanding the ecosystem and the product line</h2>
  <p>There was nothing to audit, so we started by learning two things: what GM Energy was offering across its product line, and how competitors were approaching the space. I visited GM dealers, competitor EV dealerships and home generator dealers to see how home backup power actually gets sold. Between that and desk research we found GM was in an unusual position — the only company selling both EVs and home battery systems, with Tesla's EV-plus-Powerwall lineup as the only direct competitor.</p>
  <p>That fed a categorization exercise: mapping the emerging HEMS products against the customers most likely to want each one. It turned a catalog of chargers, storage and backup hardware into a map of <em>who each product is for</em>, which everything downstream built on. We ran service design workshops throughout, so GM stakeholders were building with us rather than reviewing from a distance.</p>
  <figure class="wmed"><img src="/assets/img/ecosystem-map.webp" alt="GM Energy customer scenarios and product ecosystem — each HEMS product mapped to the customers most likely to want it" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Customer scenarios × product ecosystem — mapping every HEMS product to who it's for, with the cross-sell sequence between them.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Personas</h2>
  <p>There was no precedent to work from, so the first three personas were largely assumptions about the kinds of customers we'd encounter. Those assumptions still needed grounding in evidence, so we built the personas from customers who had bought the closest adjacent products — solar and backup generators.</p>
  <p>To validate our assumptions we then gathered stakeholder interviews and secondary research, which resolved them into three EV-centric archetypes: <b>Markus</b> (electro-curious), <b>Tom</b> (Sierra EV buyer), and <b>Fiona</b> (other EV buyers). Dentsu's M1 segmentation sharpened the targeting: Tech Builders near-term, Smart Living long-term. A GM Financial journey workshop added Voice-of-Customer personas that pushed us to consider financing behavior explicitly.</p>
  <p>Early on we also discovered a group we hadn't accounted for: people who want home energy security because extreme weather keeps knocking their power out, and who have no interest in an electric vehicle at all. That became <b>Rhonda</b> — and for a company built around vehicles it mattered beyond the design: the energy business had customers who would never arrive through vehicle sales. That gave us the four personas we needed to build journeys specific to each one.</p>
  <p>Throughout the project we kept refining them against evidence. We pulled sentiments out of <em>every</em> usability study run since May 2023, sorted them by knowledge, cost and concerns, grouped the real test participants, and built new attribute charts from what those people had actually said — evolving them into an intimate understanding of who our customers are.</p>
  <div class="sub-label">The four personas</div>
  <div class="jgal-wrap">
    <div class="jgal">
      <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/persona-markus-full.webp" alt="Markus persona card — Tech Builder segment" loading="lazy" />
        <div class="cap"><span class="step">Tech Builder</span><h4>Markus</h4><p>Efficiency-minded project manager, frustrated by outages — wants visibility and control over home energy, considering an EV.</p></div></figure>
      <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/persona-tom-full.webp" alt="Tom persona card — Shiny Elite segment" loading="lazy" />
        <div class="cap"><span class="step">Shiny Elite</span><h4>Tom</h4><p>Finance executive and GM EV owner who wants the best of the best — premium products, reliable through blackouts.</p></div></figure>
      <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/persona-fiona-full.webp" alt="Fiona persona card — Smart Tech segment" loading="lazy" />
        <div class="cap"><span class="step">Smart Tech</span><h4>Fiona</h4><p>Research-driven Lyriq owner weighing solar and backup power — values usability, warranties, and the best possible deal.</p></div></figure>
      <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/persona-rhonda-full.webp" alt="Rhonda persona card — Smart &amp; Safe segment" loading="lazy" />
        <div class="cap"><span class="step">Smart &amp; Safe</span><h4>Rhonda</h4><p>Educator nearing retirement in blackout-prone Florida — wants dependable backup power and expert guidance, no EV interest.</p></div></figure>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)">Each card carries its segmentation lineage. Markus and Tom map to a single M1 segment each — Tech Builder and Shiny Elite. Fiona and Rhonda are blends: Smart Living + Tech Builder, and Smart Living + Safety &amp; Freedom.</p>

  
  <div class="sub-label">How they are used</div>
  <p>These four personas were an important document that guided our strategy and design going forward. Every decision was made in consideration of what these personas' goals were in their HEMS buying decisions. We shared them with the entire team and the client to make sure whatever we created would be sympathetic to their pain points and help them achieve their goals as customers. They also went into the sales guide to build empathy among the home energy consultants — something they could look to as they spoke with customers, guiding the conversation.</p>

  <h2>The service blueprint</h2>
  <p>Journey mapping started the same week as the personas — a journey is a route somebody takes, so we needed to know who — and the blueprint came out of it. At the week-four workshop we walked GM through the research, co-created the work-in-progress journeys with their team, and started blueprinting from there. The two then ran alongside each other for the next two months.</p>
  <p>What it became was a large end-to-end service blueprint covering the full lifecycle — learn, shop, buy, install, own. For every customer step it mapped the touchpoints, the frontstage staff actions, and the backstage machinery: which teams, which platforms, which support processes had to exist for that moment to work. For a company standing up commerce, an installer network, and financing all at once, the blueprint became the shared reference for what had to be built — and we kept revising it for years as the real product took shape.</p>
  <p>On the commerce journey alone we mapped 67 potential moments across 12 touchpoints, and 46 of the features that came out of it went into the approved 2024+ roadmap. We built parallel maps for public charging (36 moments) and post-purchase care (35 moments).</p>
  <div class="brief-only" style="max-width:820px;margin:0 auto">
    <h2>The foundation</h2>
    <p>Every track above uses the same service blueprint and journey system, built in 2022 and revised continuously since. On the commerce journey alone: 67 moments mapped across 12 touchpoints, 46 features carried into the approved roadmap, and 23+ usability tests run against it.</p>
  </div>
  <figure class="jmap brief-keep"><img src="/assets/img/journey-service-map.webp" alt="The master service blueprint — the full HEMS customer lifecycle at scale" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The master service blueprint — the full HEMS lifecycle across phases, touchpoints, and backstage systems.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>Nobody had mapped this category yet, so waiting for solid data would have stalled the project. We wrote our assumptions down instead and kept going, treating the blueprint as <em>something to correct rather than something finished</em>. Each assumption became a thing to test: usability studies and surveys went after the riskiest ones, and we folded in new research as it arrived — the audience segmentation especially — which either confirmed what we'd sketched or overturned it.</p>
  <p>The master map covered the whole system, but it was too big to design from directly. I pulled individual columns out of it — checkout and financing especially — and expanded each into its own journey map, tied to the personas so it showed not just <em>what</em> happened at a step but <em>who</em> was there and what they were dealing with. That's the version the team could actually work from.</p>
  <figure class="jmap"><img src="/assets/img/checkout-journey.webp" alt="The checkout section highlighted on the master blueprint and expanded into a detailed journey in a working session" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The checkout section spotlighted on the master blueprint and worked into its own detailed journey in a live session — where the checkout design and wireframing began.</figcaption></figure>

  <div class="sub-label">One blueprint, many journeys</div>
  <div class="journey-split">
    <div class="journey-imgs">
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/journey-checkout.webp" alt="Detailed checkout and financing journey" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Checkout &amp; financing</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/journey-combined.webp" alt="EV and HEMS combined ownership journey" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>EV + HEMS combined</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/journey-ev.webp" alt="GM EV owner journey" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The EV owner's path</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="/assets/img/journey-simplified.webp" alt="Simplified stakeholder journey" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Simplified stakeholder view</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="journey-text">
      <h3>Each version got more specific as we learned more about the customer.</h3>
      <p>We started with one checkout and financing path and ended up with several: a combined route for customers buying an EV and home energy together, a separate one for EV owners, and a simplified version for stakeholders who needed the shape of it without every step.</p>
      <p>The same blueprint could be drawn in detail or at a high level depending on who needed to read it, and each new version took in whatever the latest research had told us.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="sub-label">The maps kept going: 2022 → 2025</div>
  <p>We were still revising them three years after the first blueprint. The current version puts personas, pain points, CRM actions and opportunities on one canvas.</p>
  <figure class="jmap"><img src="/assets/img/hems/journey-v2-2025.webp" alt="GM Energy for Home Journey Map V2, May 2025" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The 2025 V2 journey map — pre-purchase, purchase, and post-purchase with main and supporting flows.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="jgal-wrap"><div class="jgal" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)">
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/journey-integrated-2025.webp" alt="Integrated HEMS journey mega-map, October 2025" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>Integrated journey</h4><p>Phases, persona swimlanes, pains, and opportunities.</p></div></figure>
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/journey-ev-2025.webp" alt="GM EV journey mega-map, September 2025" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>EV journey</h4><p>Order-to-ownership for the vehicle side.</p></div></figure>
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/journey-combined-2025.webp" alt="Combined EV + HEMS journey, September 2025" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>EV + HEMS combined</h4><p>Fiona's world — both purchases as one arc.</p></div></figure>
  </div></div>
  <div class="sub-label">Information architecture</div>
  <p>With the journey settled we mapped the structure — every page of the site, before any of it existed.</p>
  <figure class="wmed"><img src="/assets/img/hems/sitemap.webp" alt="Basic sitemap — the full site page tree" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The sitemap: GM → GM Energy → For Home, with Genie, Live Demo, cart, and payment flows.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>One map, four ways through it</h2>
  <p>Where the purchase opportunities sat depended on where the customer started. Mapping each persona's route through the journeys is what showed us where those opportunities actually lived.</p>
  <p>Each detailed journey is a section of the service map rebuilt around a specific buyer — same underlying map, different touchpoints, different order of events.</p>
  <p>Working through the four personas produced <b>three journeys</b> — HEMS, EV, and a combined one for customers buying both together. Some personas share a journey but enter and leave at different points; some cross from one into another partway through. <em>Hover a persona to see their path.</em></p>

