  <h2>Designing for trust: financing inside checkout</h2>
  <p>Financing was the hardest part. A HEMS purchase is a big financial decision, so GM Financial had to sit inside the journey rather than beside it: a two-step prequalification where customers first see their buying power, then trade more information — including a full social security number — for real rates, terms, and monthly payments.</p>
  <p>Asking for a social security number online is a lot to ask, and testing told us how it felt from the other side. Participants said an unexplained SSN field made a company seem "scammy." They preferred a concrete rate, term and monthly payment over the abstraction of "buying power." And being asked to prequalify twice with no explanation lost their confidence — <em>"I don't understand why I have to get prequalified again."</em> So we explained ourselves at each sensitive step: why we were asking, what happened next, what they got in return, and what it did to their credit.</p>
  <p>Getting there took the team into the room together, repeatedly — dedicated GMF &amp; Checkout working sessions in Detroit and a second round in Austin, on top of the sprint-by-sprint iteration.</p>
  <p>Behind the visible flow sat a lot of combinations. Checkout wasn't one path but a matrix: hardware only, hardware plus installation, with or without a down payment, in states where financing was available and states where it wasn't, at full approval, partial approval, or decline — plus agent-assisted orders placed on the customer's behalf. Each permutation needed its own designed states and its own validation, and we ran user-acceptance testing across roughly thirty scenarios to prove the flow held together.</p>
