  <div class="act-kicker" id="part-04"><span>Part 04</span>Keep it honest</div>
  <h2>Building a testing practice</h2>
  <p>After launch, research became a standing operation. I helped structure it as two tracks, so the team could both validate designs in progress and evaluate what had already shipped:</p>
  <figure class="jmap"><img src="/assets/img/hems/testing-process.webp" alt="The two-track testing process canvas — formal and informal approaches plus the 2025 Testing Quickstart Guide" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The practice on one canvas: the formal readout track, the informal rapid-test track, and the Testing Quickstart Guide that lets anyone on the team spin up a study. Behind it: quarterly usability check-ins and 45+ UAT sessions producing ~1,900 captures before launches.</figcaption></figure>
  <table class="track-tbl">
    <tr><th></th><th>Formal</th><th>Informal</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Intent</td><td>Retroactively evaluate shipped experiences</td><td>Proactively validate solutions mid-exploration</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Scope</td><td>Comprehensive · fully built or hi-fi prototypes</td><td>Narrow · lower fidelity, one question at a time</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Velocity</td><td>~1 per month</td><td>3–4 per month</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Planning</td><td>A month ahead · ~4-week lifecycle</td><td>Coincides with the design release schedule</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p style="margin-top:24px">Dozens of studies ran across both tracks — recruited through UserTesting.com, moderated and unmoderated, desktop and mobile, with quarterly check-ins to the client. A sample of what changed because of them:</p>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><b>Forced sign-in removed from promo codes</b><span>Testers found requiring an account to redeem a code unusual for a one-time purchase, and several read it as a privacy grab.</span></li>
    <li><b>Plain language beat specifications</b><span>A "What's Included" section outperformed technical product specs 7 of 10 as the thing that helped people understand what they were buying.</span></li>
    <li><b>Navigation simplified after a card sort</b><span>Given 24 energy products to group, most testers used four categories or fewer — far simpler than the taxonomy we'd built.</span></li>
    <li><b>Order status was effectively unfindable</b><span>The account dashboard card was almost universally overlooked; every participant went hunting elsewhere for their order.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p>We also acted as a critical partner to GM's own analytics team — reviewing their survey and heat-mapping studies and returning assessments and recommendations. One finding shaped the PDP directly: product specifications drew the highest engagement of any content (31%), yet only 45% of users ever scrolled far enough to see it.</p>
  <blockquote class="pull">"Overall, the feedback highlights a well-designed process with room for minor improvements in clarity, education and support."<cite>Post-launch usability readout — financing flow, N=16</cite></blockquote>

  <h2>Designing the ownership phase</h2>
  <p>In late 2023 the engagement expanded to cover what happens after the sale, with an explicit brief: give the ownership phase the same design attention as the purchase phase.</p>
  <p>That meant journey mapping and service blueprinting for everything after checkout — installation handoff, warranty, support, returns, escalation — across a partner ecosystem where GM owned the brand but not every touchpoint: installers, solar partners, logistics providers, and an EV Concierge contact center whose agents needed training plans built around the new journeys.</p>

  <div class="sub-label">Where the transaction ends</div>
  <p>A HEMS order is hardware plus licensed electrical work, and often a loan on top — so even the receipt had to represent all three at once. "Labor &amp; Installation Total" sits as a peer to "Product Total" rather than as a line item under it, and the financing variant replaces "Order Total" with "Total Expected Financing with GM Financial" — a different promise to the customer, and a different document.</p>
  <div class="live-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr">
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/care-invoice-financing.webp" alt="GM Energy invoice — financing variant, with placeholder values" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Financing variant — the closing line is an expectation, not a charge.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/care-invoice-card.webp" alt="GM Energy invoice — card payment variant, with placeholder values" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Card variant — the same document, resolved as a completed order total.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <div class="sub-label">And where it goes wrong</div>
  <p>We mapped the unhappy paths the same way. The returns and cancellations journey runs six steps across two sub-phases, tracking what the customer is doing and thinking at each one, the feeling we anticipated, the pain points, and the opportunity that came out of it — with the concierge escalation tiers mapped alongside, since who picks up the phone changes what the experience can be.</p>
  <figure class="wmed"><img src="/assets/img/hems/care-returns-journey.webp" alt="Returns and cancellations journey map — doing, thinking, anticipated feeling, pain points and opportunities across six steps" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Returns &amp; cancellations. The pain points row is the useful one: tracking that isn't accurate, customers without a printer for a return label, confusion over who to pay when a third party did the install.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>It also meant designing the feedback system itself. We mapped customer-satisfaction survey triggers across the entire ecosystem — post-sale, post-install, post-support-call, per partner — and then did the less obvious work of pruning them, prioritizing which moments genuinely warranted asking so customers weren't buried under surveys from four companies about the same purchase.</p>
  <p>The same logic applied to the messages themselves. Every email the care team could send was inventoried, assigned an audience, and ranked into three priority tiers — because at launch an agent would be picking these from a template library by hand, and the first tier had to be the one that actually mattered.</p>
  <figure class="wmed"><img src="/assets/img/hems/care-email-taxonomy.webp" alt="Customer care email taxonomy — messages grouped into three priority tiers with their audiences" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>The care email set, tiered. Note the audience column: seven of the twenty-five never reach the customer at all — they coordinate the installation, logistics and administration partners around them.</figcaption></figure>

  
  <div class="act-kicker" id="part-05"><span>Part 05</span>Supporting tracks</div>
  <h2>Energy Genie: helping people work out what to buy</h2>
  <p>A home energy system is a considered purchase — testers told us they spend weeks or months researching one. They wanted to explore on their own, and they were open to using a tool as long as it was accurate, unbiased and actually personalized.</p>
  <p>What existed was a menu-driven assistant that could answer prompts and pass you to a human, but couldn't help you decide <em>what to buy</em>. We looked at how other companies handled it — Ford's EV lifestyle quiz, Bosch's smart-home configurator, Logitech's setup finder — and at what home-battery owners were saying in their own communities.</p>
  <div class="gseq gseq--full">
    <figure><div class="shot shot--nat shot--bare"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v1-00-flow-map.webp" alt="The shipped Energy Genie survey laid out as a flow — intro, EV charging, energy storage, solar, savings and installation, ending on the recommendation" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The shipped survey end to end. Each row is one section of the questionnaire, left to right in the order a customer answered them.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <div class="sub-label">Genie 1.0</div>
  <p>The first version was a questionnaire. It ran in five sections — EV charging, energy storage, solar, savings and installation — with Previous and Next at the bottom of every step. It calculated as you went: pick your square footage and the appliances you'd want running during an outage, and it estimated your daily and outage energy use, then matched that total to a product bundle. The page explained it as three steps.</p>
  <ol class="phases">
    <li><i>01</i>Answer some questions</li>
    <li><i>02</i>Get a personalized energy solution</li>
    <li><i>03</i>Add to cart &amp; checkout</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="lg-afford"><span>Interactive</span>The tall screens scroll on their own — hover to take over.</div>
  <div class="gseq gseq--3">
    <figure><div class="shot lg-scroll lg-drift"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v1-01-landing.webp" alt="Energy Genie 1.0 landing page" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Landing page</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><div class="shot lg-scroll lg-drift"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v1-02-question.webp" alt="Energy Genie 1.0 survey step — selecting appliances to power during an outage, with live kWh estimates" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>A question step</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><div class="shot lg-scroll lg-drift"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v1-03-results.webp" alt="Energy Genie 1.0 recommendation page with personalized reasoning" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The recommendation</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>We kept testing it after launch. Half the testers found the vehicle-confirmation step redundant with the one before it, so we cut it. Half found the "Goals" page unclear next to "Priorities," so we merged the two. The recommendation page itself tested well — 80% rated it 4 or 5 out of 5, and called the personalized reasoning and the outage-risk section thoughtful. But the same testers, including the ones who rated it highly, wanted two or three options instead of a single answer, and 60% wanted installation detail before they'd commit. Those two gaps are what we took into the next version.</p>

  <div class="sub-label">Genie 2.0 — two ways in</div>
  <p>For the second version we moved Genie out of its own page and into a panel that could open anywhere on the site. We mapped six entry points — the homepage and nav, the shop-all listing, product pages, public charging, installation and financing — and wrote a different set of opening prompts for each. On a product page it asked "Good for outages?" or "Don't know how to install this?" On the listing page, "Too many options? Let's narrow it down." On financing, "Understand your monthly payments."</p>
  <div class="gseq gseq--full">
    <figure><div class="shot shot--nat"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v2-00-entry-points.webp" alt="Genie 2.0 entry points diagram — six site surfaces, each with prompts written for that context" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Six entry points, each with prompts written for where the customer already was.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>From there we designed two routes through it. Both start by collecting what the customer needs, but they differ in when the recommendation appears.</p>

  <p><strong>The guided path</strong> is for someone who doesn't know where to start — the entry prompt says exactly that: "Don't know where to start? Let us guide you!" It asks structured questions about where you live, your monthly bill and your energy goal. Under every set of options we left a free-text field open, so a question that didn't fit the choices wouldn't trap anyone. It ends on one recommendation with the reasoning attached.</p>
  <ol class="phases">
    <li><i>01</i>Collect customer needs</li>
    <li><i>02</i>Answer outstanding questions</li>
    <li><i>03</i>Recommend product</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="gseq gseq--2">
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v2-guided-01-goal.webp" alt="Genie 2.0 guided path — an energy-goal question with three choices and a free-text field beneath" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>A question step, with the free-text field underneath</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v2-guided-02-reco.webp" alt="Genie 2.0 guided path — the recommendation, with the customer classified and the chat input still open" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The recommendation it ends on</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <p><strong>The chat path</strong> starts from whatever the customer types. Genie reads it back as a summary first, so they can correct it before anything else happens. It pulls in property data, local utility information and outage history, which lets the answers be about that specific house — in the prototype a customer says their biggest worry is a storm-related outage, and Genie replies that their area had repeated outages last spring. This route reaches a recommendation earlier than the guided one, then keeps adjusting it: two products side by side instead of one, a plain explanation when the customer asks what V2H means, and an energy profile chart that redraws as they answer more.</p>
  <ol class="phases">
    <li><i>01</i>Collect customer needs</li>
    <li><i>02</i>Fine-tune recommendations</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="lg-afford"><span>Interactive</span>The full conversation — it scrolls on its own, hover to take over.</div>
  <div class="gseq gseq--1">
    <figure><div class="shot lg-scroll lg-drift"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/v2-chat-01-full.webp" alt="Genie 2.0 chat path, end to end — free-form question, read-back, structured details, two product options, a V2H explainer, and an energy profile chart" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The chat path, start to finish</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>Showing two products, and leaving the reasoning open to questions, was our answer to what the testing had told us. The two routes aren't separate products either: the conversation can hand off to the guided questions, the guided path keeps a chat field open the whole way through, and either one can pass the customer to a human Home Energy Consultant.</p>
  <div class="gseq gseq--full">
    <figure><div class="shot shot--nat"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie-pathways.webp" alt="Genie 2.0 Guided Experience Pathways concept diagram" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The concept both routes came from — three customers moving between chat and survey on their own way to a recommendation.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <div class="sub-label">What it would have been worth</div>
  <p>We framed the research around two questions instead of one: how does Genie 2.0 help the customer, and how does it help the business. The board below splits them, with the analytics layer in between and arrows running both ways — experience data informs strategy, strategy informs experience. Most of this framing and the synthesis that came out of it was our senior designers' work; I worked through it with them, mainly checking that it held together as a direction.</p>
  <div class="gseq gseq--full">
    <figure><div class="shot shot--tall lg-scroll lg-drift"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/biz-01-themes.webp" alt="Genie 2.0 research themes board — front-end customer questions above an analytics layer, back-end business questions below, linked by a two-way loop" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Front end asks what would make a customer use it, or not. Back end asks which teams the resulting data serves — down to legal.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>The case that came out of it went past buying. The argument was that every conversation is a small piece of research: which products draw real interest, where people hesitate, which terms they find unclear or intimidating. The value proposition put it as an assistant layer that "captures real customer questions in their own words — offering ongoing voice-of-customer feedback without surveys."</p>
  <p>Everything else follows from that. Interest grouped by location shows where demand is building — storage in zip codes that lose power often. Drop-off points become a diagnostic: which step loses people, and whether it's pricing, installation or comparing products. The inputs Genie already collects — home type, energy priorities, location — feed lead scoring instead of disappearing with the session. And because it's a conversation rather than a fixed page, it can test ideas: gauge whether customers want smart panels or V2G before any of it goes to market.</p>
  <div class="gseq gseq--full">
    <figure><div class="shot shot--nat"><img src="/assets/img/hems/genie/biz-02-insights.webp" alt="Genie 2.0 insights synthesis — the business half, grouped into seven columns from overall company benefit through product strategy" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The business half of the synthesis, in seven columns. Every card cites its source — our own surveys and analytics alongside Baymard guidelines.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>None of it shipped. Genie 2.0 was scoped and prototyped and then put on hold — it sits on the program status board between two other paused workstreams, with its usability test still dated TBD. The Genie that's live is still the survey.</p>


  <h2>Executive dashboard</h2>
  <p>Leadership needed to see how the business was performing without asking the team for a status update. The executive dashboard became one of the largest efforts alongside the storefront — a business-facing view of funnel performance and program health at a glance.</p>
  <figure class="wmed"><img src="/assets/img/hems/dash-wip.webp" alt="Executive dashboard design in progress in Figma — annotation card over a line-graph widget" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--hair);display:block" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption>Work in progress — an annotation layer lets analysts pin commentary directly onto the data widgets.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="jgal-wrap" style="margin-top:22px"><div class="jgal">
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/dash-map-light.webp" alt="US map component exploration — light theme" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>Map exploration — light</h4><p>Regional install and sales density, editorial-styled for executive readouts.</p></div></figure>
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/dash-map-dark.webp" alt="US map component exploration — dark theme" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>Map exploration — dark</h4><p>The same component tuned for the dashboard's dark data-panel context.</p></div></figure>
  </div></div>

  
  <h2>The sales guide: seven versions to get it right</h2>
  <p>Nobody asked for the sales guide. The sales team needed the same journey the site was built on, in a form they could use in a conversation, so I made one. I stayed its primary point of contact throughout, and it was adopted across the whole GME sales organization — seven versions between April 2024 and December 2025, as consultant feedback turned it from a graphics-heavy 48-page deck into a step-by-step 35-page guide.</p>
  <div class="jgal-wrap"><div class="jgal" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)">
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/salesguide-p1.webp" alt="GM Energy Sales Guide cover" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>Cover — Dec 2025 edition</h4></div></figure>
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/salesguide-p5.webp" alt="Basic Sitemap page — every page of the site at a glance" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>Site at a glance</h4></div></figure>
    <figure class="jcard"><img src="/assets/img/hems/salesguide-p6.webp" alt="Explore Products flowchart page" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><h4>Guided flowcharts</h4></div></figure>
  </div></div>

  <div class="brief-keep">
  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>GM Energy went from no ecommerce presence to a launched, financed, installable product experience. It also came away with the things built around it: a service blueprint the organization designs against, personas grounded in evidence that changed who the business thinks it serves, a two-track testing program feeding into every release, an ownership experience worked out as carefully as the sale, and a sales guide that puts the journey in every seller's hands.</p>
  <p>I stayed with it from the start through launch, and through the years of improvements, testing and new workstreams that came after.</p>
  <div class="live-grid live-grid--wide">
    <figure><div class="shot lg-menus"><img src="/assets/img/hems/live-menu-closed.webp" alt="Live gmenergy.gm.com homepage" loading="lazy"><img class="mstate" src="/assets/img/hems/live-menu-ev-charging.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy"><img class="mstate" src="/assets/img/hems/live-menu-vehicle-to-home.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy"><img class="mstate" src="/assets/img/hems/live-menu-home-energy.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The live site — mega menus in motion.</figcaption></figure>
